introduction/introduction.tex:The location of the tricritical point has been calculated by making assumptions about how to extrapolate to finite \baryochemical from \baryochemical = 0, and these calculations are based on models rather than being solidly rooted in QCD \cite{Stephanov:2007fk}.  \Fref{TricriticalPoint} shows model predictions for the location of the tricritical point, demonstrating that there is still no consensus. %The red circles correspond to the freeze-out points where the medium cools down and breaks apart for heavy ion collisions with various energies.
introduction/introduction.tex:There are two different classes of theoretical frameworks, those which attempt to describe the QGP describing the interactions between quarks and gluons from first principles and those which make assumptions about the QGP and are a more phenomenological description of the data.  Theories which attempt to describe the interactions of quarks and gluons from first principles, such as energy loss models and the Color Glass Condensate, are more solidly founded in QCD, however, assumptions about the initial state and hadronization are made to relate these theories to data.  Statistical models and hydrodynamical models are dependent on assumptions about the medium created.  These classes of models both depend on the medium reaching equilibrium, at least locally.
introduction/introduction.tex:%In addition, many models which are not based on QCD but rather are based on assumptions about the way that a QGP should behave are used to describe the data.  Thermal models assume that the medium is in chemical and thermal equilibrium and therefore that the relative yields of particle species can be described statistically.  There have been extensions of these models to include non-equilibrium processes.  Generally these models do not attempt to describe the production of high momentum particles but rather only the production of low momentum, or bulk, particles.
introduction/introduction.tex:%Hydrodynamical models are frequently used to describe heavy ion collisions.  These models describe any fluid and therefore could describe both a hadron gas and a QGP.  Their biggest inherent weakness is an assumption that the medium is in thermal equilibrium.  In addition many calculations have been done assuming that the viscosity of the fluid is zero.  There are indeed some indications that the viscosity of the fluid may be very low \cite{Back:2004je,Adcox:2004mh,Arsene:2004fa,Adams:2005dq} and the medium formed at RHIC has been termed a ``Perfect Fluid.''
introduction/introduction.tex:%A family of phenomenological models is based on the assumption that, if a QGP has been formed, instead of quarks hadronizing as they do in a vacuum, they can combine with other quarks in the medium to produce hadrons.  This is called Recombination or Coalescence.  These models qualitatively reproduce the many aspects of the data, however, generally fail to quantitatively reproduce the data.
introduction/introduction.tex:It was predicted that a QGP would be dense and therefore that production of high-\pT hadrons would be suppressed because energy would be lost by fast moving partons traversing the medium \cite{Bass:1999zq}.  When there is a hard scattering of two partons in a QGP, the dense medium should slow or stop the parton, a process called jet quenching.  The partons that escape the medium are assumed to be biased towards the surface of the medium.  A theoretical calculation of the distribution of the origins hadrons produced relative to an 8 \GeV hadron in a dense medium are shown in \Fref{SurfaceBias}, demonstrating that the measured high-\pT hadrons are expected to be biased towards those produced near the surface.  This would lead to \RAA $<$ 1 because there would be fewer high-\pT partons in an \AplusA collision than in a \pp collision.
