introduction/introduction.tex:%If a QGP is formed and it reaches thermal equilibrium, statistical models describing the relative numbers of particles expected at equilibrium could describe the data.  These models, described in greater detail in \cite{BraunMunzinger:2003zd}, use the relative yields of measured particle spectra and generally assume light quarks to be in equilibrium.  \Fref{ThermalFits} shows that fit of data from \Au collisions at RHIC at \sNNtwohundred describes the data well.  The data imply that the medium may be in equilibrium since they are described well by a thermal model.  An additional factor $\gamma_s$ is used in this model to determine if strange quarks have also come into equilibrium; the data imply that they have in central \Au collisions.  However, these models also describe \pp collisions at \sNNtwohundred  \cite{Adams:2005dq}.
introduction/introduction.tex:It is assumed that the azimuthal momentum anisotropy originates from the collective movement of a fluid formed at early stages of the collision.  This is the most universally accepted mechanism for the production of an azimuthal anisotropy, however, other mechanisms have been proposed \cite{Krasnitz:2002ng,Teaney:2002kn,Kopeliovich:2008nx,Boreskov:2008uy}.  In a hydrodynamical model, the initial anisotropy in the distribution of particles leads to a pressure gradient.  \Fref{HydroPressureGradient} shows theoretical calculations for the contours of constant density.  The pressure gradient is perpendicular to these lines, meaning that it is azimuthally anisotropic.  As the system evolves, the spatial azimuthal anisotropy in the distribution of particles will disappear.  The anisotropy in the momentum distribution of particles due to the pressure gradient will remain.
