introduction/introduction.tex:\Fref{v2dNdy} shows measurements of \vtwo at various collision energies compared to hydrodynamical predictions for the upper limit of \vtwo.  The x-axis is the number of particles produced per unit rapidity\footnote{Defined in \Aref{Terminology}} divided by the transverse area of overlap of the nuclei, S.  The y-axis is \vtwo divided by the eccentricity, $\epsilon$, of the overlap area.  S and $\epsilon$ are determined using a Glauber Monte Carlo model for nuclear collisions \cite{Miller:2007ri}.  This normalization leads to the cancellation of trivial geometric effects.  The predictions are from ideal hydrodynamics, meaning that the viscosity is assumed to be zero.  A non-zero viscosity would only decrease the observed \vtwo so these are upper limits for \vtwo, shown as green lines in \Fref{v2dNdy}.  If these limits are correct, then the medium created in central collisions at RHIC is very close to hydrodynamical limits and therefore the viscosity must be very low.
introduction/introduction.tex:Since hydrodynamics assumes thermalization, the success of ideal hydrodynamics at describing many aspects of RHIC data implies that the medium is thermalized when \vtwo is formed.  \Fref{PHENIXv2Scaling} demonstrates that hadrons containing strange quarks follow the same trends for \vtwo, implying that strangeness is also thermalized.  Furthermore, not only is strangeness thermalized, but measurements of electrons from decays of hadrons including charm quarks indicate that the \vtwo of charm quarks is probably comparable to that of light quarks \cite{Adare:2006nq}.
introduction/introduction.tex:Our understanding of the QGP and the phase diagram of matter has improved significantly since the beginning of the RHIC program.  Many methods that have been used to describe a QGP qualitatively describe the features of the data, however, we do not yet have a complete quantitative description of the data.  Some methods attempt to make predictions by calculating results directly from QCD.  Provided that the approximations are valid, these theories should describe the partonic phases well, however, they are dependent on models of hadronization and interactions in a hadronic gas phase.  Our knowledge of hadronization is heavily dependent upon experimental data because hadronization is a non-perturbative process.  If the medium goes through a hadronic gas phase for any significant amount of time and hadrons re-interact, this would also change the properties of measured hadrons.  Either of these effects or invalid assumptions made in calculations could be responsible for deviations between QCD-based theories and experimental data.
previousresults/previousresults.tex:It is generally assumed that the \ns comes from surface-biased partons, while the \as comes from partons which have traveled through the medium.  However the kinematic ranges of studies focusing on the near- and \as overlap, so particles on \as in one study may be a trigger particle in another.
previousresults/previousresults.tex:For an intermediate \pT trigger (roughly \trigrange{3.0}{6.0}), particles on the \as are observed at lower momentum than in \pp and \dAu collisions.  Few particles are observed at \ptassoc \roughly 2 \GeV (\Fref{IconicJetQuench}), but at lower momenta the \as is broadened and develops a double-peaked structure (\Fref{LowPtCorr}).  When the momenta of the trigger and associated particles are increased, the \as reappears (\Fref{PunchThrough}); this is interpreted as a greater surface bias and a bias towards unmodified jet fragmentation.  The \ridge is the dominant feature on the \ns for intermediate \pT triggers (\Fref{IconicRidgePlot}).  It persists out to \pttrig \roughly 8 \GeV, however, a greater percentage of the particles correlated with the trigger particle are in the \jlc (\Fref{RidgeVSPTTrigVSCent}).  As \ptassoc is increased, a greater percentage of the particles correlated with the trigger particle are in the \jlc (\Fref{RidgeVSJetVSPtAssoc}); at lower \ptassoc, measurements are overwhelmed by systematic errors and precise data are unavailable.  The features observed in correlation studies are momentum dependent, presumably reflecting differences in the relative importance of various mechanisms which produce correlations as a function of \pT.
results/results.tex:These data are compared to \py.  \py is an event generator for collisions such as \ee and \pp; it is not currently able to describe heavy ion collisions.  It is a Monte Carlo generator which incorporates analytical results and QCD-based models.  The parameters have been tuned to match data \cite{Sjostrand:2006za}.  \py generally describes spectra of $\pi$, K, and p well, while other measurements are generally more sensitive to the parameters used in \py.  Version 8.1 of \py is used here.  \py 8.1 has not been tuned to RHIC data.  To determine the anticipated \jly in \py, \pp collisions are simulated.  The default settings were used except that \pthatmin was set to 0.1.  \pthat is the parameter in PYTHIA for the transverse momentum in the hard subprocess \cite{Sjostrand:2006za}.  The analysis described in \Cref{CorrMethod} is repeated except that no separation of the \jlc and the \ridge is necessary because there is no \ridge in \pp and the combinatorial background is assumed to be constant because there is assumed to be no hydrodynamical flow in \pp collisions.
results/results.tex:\Fref{JetEnergyNpart} shows the \npart dependence of \njet for \Cu and \Au collisions at \sNNsixtytwo and for \dAu, \Cu, and \Au collisions at \sNNtwohundred.  The data are compared to \py scaled by 2/3.  There is a slight increase of \njet with \npart in \Au collisions at \sNNtwohundred.  \njet in \Au collisions at \sNNtwohundred are slighly higher than those in \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred and \njet is about 50\% larger in central \Au collisions at \sNNtwohundred than in \dAu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.  This may be either because there is some modification of the \jlc in \AplusA relative to the \jlc in \dAu, because the jet spectra which lead to the \jlc are different in \AplusA and \dAu, or because some of the \ridge is misidentified as part of the \jlc.  The method for separating the \jlc and the \ridge inherently assumes that the \ridge is independent of \deta.
theory/theory.tex:The Momentum Kick Model has been used to describe the data on the collision energy dependence of the \ridge presented in this thesis.  This was not done by fitting the data from \sNNsixtytwo but by assuming that the size of the \ridge scales with the density of partons and that the density of partons scales with the density of participants \cite{WongMomKickMain}.  This energy dependence is shown in \Fref{WongMomEnergyDependence}.
theory/theory.tex:Recombination was introduced in response to a baryon enhancement in the inclusive data.  In Recombination, hadrons form by the combination of partons in the medium.  The partons may either be thermal partons, those coming from the medium, or shower partons, those coming from hard scattering.  In this model the \jlc comes from shower partons or combinations of thermal and shower partons.  The inclusive spectra were fit to determine the thermal and shower contributions to the spectra and based on these fits it was predicted that there would be no correlation observed for \myomega triggered correlations \cite{Hwa:2006vb}.  The data in \cite{bettysthesis,Abelev:2007zp} contradicted this prediction.  \cite{Chiu:2007zr} explained the discrepancy by hypothesizing that the \ridge comes from ``phantom jets'', that the \myomega trigger is actually from the \ridge, and that the \myomega triggered correlation presented in \cite{bettysthesis,Abelev:2007zp} comprises only a \ridge but no \jlc.  There are not enough statistics to separate the \jlc from the \ridge in the \myomega triggered correlation.  This model presumes, however, that there is a jet which created the \ridge; the \myomega trigger is in the \ridge for this jet.
